{
  "image_name": "59e619b979cd1a267622b7629a81f9c0.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how nanoreactors can improve catalysis through three broad kinds of effects. Overall, it shows that the shape and internal architecture of a nanoscale cavity, the way molecules move through it, and the arrangement of different catalytic sites can all control reaction efficiency and selectivity.\n\nThe top section focuses on cavity effects. A curved, confined hollow structure can enrich reactants inside the cavity, isolate different active sites, and place them close enough for spatiotemporal coupling. In other words, the nanoreactor acts as a small compartment that concentrates molecules and organizes where catalytic events happen.\n\nThe middle section highlights mass-transfer and diffusion effects. Different channel shapes appear to regulate how two reactants enter, move through, or become separated within the nanoreactor. Transfer channels and pore geometry seem to guide diffusion, helping tune access to the catalytic interior and improving transport.\n\nThe bottom section shows active-site synergy. Two distinct active sites positioned within the same nanoreactor can work together, enabling cooperative catalysis and tandem reactions. A reaction pathway is illustrated in which reactants are converted through intermediates at successive sites to form a final product, emphasizing how spatially coordinated sites can promote multistep transformations.",
